model {
  extend basalt.build {
    metadata {
      constraint "A release that changes nothing about a container leaves it alone. A Worker keeps its recorded class namespaces readable across an update while every class it binds now has a recorded namespace and was bound the same way before, even when one of them is a class another Worker hosts, so a container attached to a class it hosts does not plan as changed. A class it starts to host is new, also under a name it used to bind from another Worker, and a binding that moves to a different Worker is a different namespace; inside a dependency cycle the other Worker's name is taken from the Worker's resolved settings when the binding is a plain entry there, and a name that cannot be known is never taken for the Worker's own, so it promises nothing. A class name bound in two ways, hosted and from another Worker or from more than one other Worker, promises nothing either. An entry for a class that is no longer bound stays readable until the map is recorded again, so retiring a class does not plan the root container. After an interrupted Worker update the planner hands over the attempted bindings, so this check can then pass once for a class that was not bound before. Container reconciliation treats an empty command or entrypoint and the private network and default runtime the platform fills in on readback as not set, so an update with nothing to change sends neither a modify nor a rollout, while a changed image, resource, command, entrypoint or network still rolls out and a changed attachment still replaces the application. The change rides in the pinned Alchemy release's dependency patch, so an Alchemy upgrade carries it forward or retires it explicitly."
    }
  }
}
